{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261112T052204Z-1e0cc1ba", "ts": "2026-11-12T05:25:11Z", "actor": {"worktree": ".claude/worktrees/agent-a76c0614686964c8c", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "feature/asks-last", "build": "9e93e8bdf2b4"}, "body": {"base_commit": "ec6557bf520c4981ba97d68e13c0d8d765ceda76", "base_tree": "9d7ac23fbc6ce5eeaa736b4f1c0b142b514a2904", "head_tree": "d8ac3ad6dd26bf57687d7914a01e74753fb7979b", "head_commit": "29849fd61bace80fa8240c47f18e9586fb98f703", "dispatch_commit": "29849fd61bace80fa8240c47f18e9586fb98f703", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/standing_block.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/session-digest.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "plugin/skills/methodology/SKILL.md", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_advisory_canary.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_complete_turn.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_empty_tasks.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_garbage.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_no_fields.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_null_message.json", "tests/test_plugin_methodology_digest.py", "tests/test_stats.py", "tests/test_stop_block_facts.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/changes/asks-last.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/standing_block.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/session-digest.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "plugin/skills/methodology/SKILL.md", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_advisory_canary.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_complete_turn.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_empty_tasks.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_garbage.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_no_fields.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_null_message.json", "tests/test_plugin_methodology_digest.py", "tests/test_stats.py", "tests/test_stop_block_facts.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/changes/asks-last.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A quoted close-then-asks layout that the turn does not follow with its own one-line close is still read as the turn's close, so it defers the reflection and Critic gates", "recommendation": "Class. This is the member of the class R-1 (rev-20261112T043311Z-004908bc) named that its fix did not reach, and it is new information, not a re-raise: the accept reason says the class is closed ('blockquote, indented code, nested fence all covered'), but the fix only voids the reading when a one-line close FOLLOWS the header. Why it broke: `_opens_asks` strips `>` and leading whitespace as decoration, and `_lines` strips each raw line, so a blockquoted or indented header opens the asks block. Everything after that header is then the block and is never read. If no close-shaped line follows (the turn ends on prose, on a question, or on the older three-paragraph close, none of which `_close_line` matches), `_one_line_close` returns the quoted line before the header. Example: `The new shape:\\n\\n> `RUNNING` \u00b7 `DO NOT CLEAR` \u2014 x\\n\\n> **Needs you:**\\n> 1. ask\\n\\nShall I adopt it?` reads as RUNNING / DO NOT CLEAR, and `gates.turn_declares_in_flight` returns (True, ...), so this turn's session-end gates defer. Before this change, the same turn had no close on its last line and the gates blocked. That breaks the documented ladder ('the permissive direction is taken ONLY on a clearly present verdict'). Every R-1 test, at both layers (`test_a_close_after_a_quoted_layout_yields_no_verdict`, `test_a_blockquoted_layout_then_a_safe_close_defers_nothing`), appends SAFE_LINE, so none of them tests this shape. Fix it in one place: decide from the RAW line (before stripping) whether a header is quoted. A header line that starts with `>` or is indented four or more spaces opens nothing, just as a fenced header opens nothing. Alternatively, require every non-blank line after the header to be part of an ask. Then pin each quoting form (blockquote, indented code) followed by NO close at the hook layer, so that it defers nothing.", "files": ["plugin/lib/standing_block.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The rule 'an asks block forbids the three-paragraph reading' is copied into three readers instead of having one owner", "recommendation": "Class, not instance. It broke because `_one_line_close` returns None for two different states: 'no one-line close, so try the older form' and 'there is an asks block but the close before it is invalid, so read nothing'. Each public reader then has to tell those apart with a second, separate parse (`_has_asks`, which re-runs `_lines` and `_asks_start`). `verdict_line`, `disposition` and `clear_verdict` each repeat `one = _one_line_close(text); if one is not None: ...; if _has_asks(text): return <empty>; <three-paragraph fallback>`. The ambiguous state 'two headers' is stored as the index-shaped sentinel `-1`, which `_has_asks` counts as 'has asks' and `_one_line_close` catches only through `start <= 0`. That gives four signals (one-line close found, asks start/none/ambiguous, the fence flag before the header, the three-paragraph form) and three copies of the order they are checked in. Consequence: these three copies are what stop asks content from reaching the verdict, the protection R-1's fix depends on. If a fourth reader is added, or one of these is refactored, without the `_has_asks` step, it reads the older form's block across the asks again. `closing_block` is public and its docstring does not say it is invalid on a turn with asks. Fix by construction: make one private `_read_close(text)` that returns a single reading (form = one-line | asks-void | three-paragraph | none, plus disposition, verdict and line), parse once, and have every public reader consume that answer. Replace the `-1` sentinel with an explicit state. Expect the existing guard tests to stay green unchanged.", "files": ["plugin/lib/standing_block.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "digest-behavior-inventory row A8 still says the asks go 'just above' the close, the acceptance criterion for the digest this change moved", "recommendation": "Instance. `.prawduct/artifacts/digest-behavior-inventory.md` A8 ('Closing line shape and labels; the asks above it ... restates every ask, numbered, just above it') describes the digest's closing section. That section now puts the asks after the close under `**Needs you:**` (session-digest.md 'Closing the turn'). The inventory exists to be the acceptance criterion for digest prose scrubs and the case list for evals. Anyone running the next scrub or writing the A8 eval case from it would judge the new digest as failing A8, or would restore the old placement. Update A8's title and Observable column to the close-then-asks shape (`**Needs you:**` header, asks last, the reason never pointing by position). This file is outside the diff, which is how it was missed. Search `.prawduct/artifacts/` (excluding archive/ and plans) for 'above it'/'just above' once more while you are there.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/digest-behavior-inventory.md", "plugin/methodology/session-digest.md"], "fid": "R-3"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 3, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "close-pointer can count a negated or elided-object comparative as a pointer", "recommendation": "Unlike `self_citation_match`, `positional_pointer_match` applies no denial-clause check. So 'nothing above needs you' records points-above, and an elided comparative such as 'costs are above, for now' also matches, because the comma tail fires. The check only records, and only by kind, so no gate or decision turns on a stray count. No edit is wanted unless the stats row is used to judge the guide.", "files": ["plugin/lib/standing_block.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: no plan claims this branch, so no governed_by disposition was recorded to reconcile", "recommendation": "No disposition to judge. The work was steered by the two recorded 2026-11-11 owner rulings, and the diff matches them: asks come last under a fixed header, the reader learns the trailing block, asks are restated after a Stop block, and close-pointer records only. I checked the api-contract Direction norms (additive-first, the exit-code contract) against the diff and found no departure. The new Stop check records and never blocks, and the stats row is additive. If this work is ever planned, seed `governed_by:` with `prawduct-hook jurisdiction`.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Two regexes for the same positional-pointer concept", "recommendation": "`_SELF_CITATIONS`' `above-below` form and `_POSITIONAL_POINTERS` both detect 'is above/below' at a clause end, in independently written patterns. They already differ ('above is for you' matches only the new one). This is documented as intended, since the two checks record different things, but a later fix to one tail will not reach the other. Optionally build `above-below` from `_POSITIONAL_TAIL`. No edit is owed.", "files": ["plugin/lib/standing_block.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "positional_pointer_match copies self_citation_match's 'slice the reason after the verdict' block", "recommendation": "Both functions do find-verdict-on-verdict_line, guard with an 'unreachable' branch, then slice. A small `_reason(text, verdict)` helper would give that one home. This would fold naturally into the single-reading refactor in the first finding. It is small, so no separate edit is owed.", "files": ["plugin/lib/standing_block.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Test comments anchor to a review finding id and review history", "recommendation": "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py says 'The class R-1 named, not its one example' and '(was SAFE TO CLEAR before the asks-last review round ...)'. R-1 is a per-review id that the next review renumbers. Delete those clauses and keep the property the test asserts. These are test comments that do not ship to consumers, so no edit is required.", "files": ["tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The 'Answering a block' instruction sits at the end of the long Stop-hook paragraph", "recommendation": "Framework Check 9 (Cumulative Health): the session-hygiene Stop-hook paragraph now carries the deferral rule, three record checks, the read position, and the block-answer rule with its new asks exception, in one line. The block reason (`STOP_BLOCK_ANSWER`) is the surface an agent actually acts on, and a test pins both carriers to each other, so the effect is small. Splitting 'Answering a block' into its own paragraph would make it easier to scan.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Two test comments still call the confirmed follow-ups 'inferred ... and put to them'", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: b6b3044b recorded the owner's confirmation ('1. Yes 2. Yes') and updated only the two oracle records, not the comments that commit 03c6114c had marked 'inferred' under R-2. tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py:1222 ('inferred from the owner's 2026-11-11 ruling') and tests/test_v5_methodology.py:1712 ('inferred from the owner's ruling and put to them') now understate the authority behind the Stop-block asks exception. They are test comments that nothing reads, so this is not a finding. If they are touched again, cite `.prawduct/owner-rulings.md` rather than saying whether the ruling was inferred or confirmed, so the comment cannot go stale again. Per core.md, 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees'.", "files": ["tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: the change record's list of reader guards predates the R-1 fix", "recommendation": "In .prawduct/changes/asks-last.md, the 'The reader' bullets say 'Nothing inside the block is read, not even a close-shaped last line'. Since 03c6114c, `_one_line_close` does read the block for close-shaped lines outside a fence, and any such line voids the verdict. The list also leaves out the rule that a header inside a code fence is ignored. The authoritative owners (the standing_block module docstring, and the 'Only the last line counts' sentence in session-hygiene.md) both state the current behaviour. The narrative never reaches the consumer note, and no operator acts on it, so it is a narration one revision short: it clears no Records Pass bar. No edit is required. If the fragment is touched before release, adding one bullet for the fence rule and the void-on-later-close rule would make it true.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/asks-last.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass coverage: both oracle files rated, none clears a bar", "recommendation": "Set covered: .prawduct/changes/asks-last.md and the 2026-11-11 additions to .prawduct/owner-rulings.md. Checked against the tree:\n- Consumer note: matches stats, which renders a `close-pointer` row under observed only, with kinds in by_gate_reason.\n- The telemetry-row pin, in both directions: test_stop_block_facts.\n- 'one test reads both' carriers: TestABlockSaysHowToAnswerIt with assert_block_says_how_to_answer.\n- The header pinned to both prose carriers: test_each_carrier_names_exactly_the_asks_header.\n- 'inferred then confirmed': matches the owner's recorded '1. Yes 2. Yes'.\nNo ship, misleads-into-action or merge-stopping falsehood found. No edit is wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/asks-last.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "oid": "O-9"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `feature/asks-last`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": "ec6557bf520c4981ba97d68e13c0d8d765ceda76", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/changes/asks-last.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/session-digest.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "plugin/skills/methodology/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "dispatched_at": "2026-11-12T05:22:08Z"}}

